1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,420
Section 4

2
00:00:01,420 --> 00:00:02,485
OK,

3
00:00:02,485 --> 00:00:09,820
so we've been looking at how man-made changes in our environment can affect wildlife.

4
00:00:09,820 --> 00:00:13,360
Now I'll discuss a particular example.

5
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:16,940
Let's take a look at mercury.

6
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:24,643
Mercury's one of the 120 or so elements that make up all matter,

7
00:00:24,643 --> 00:00:28,197
and it has the symbol Hg.

8
00:00:28,197 --> 00:00:31,160
It's a shiny, silvery substance.

9
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:35,084
You may have seen it in old-fashioned thermometers,

10
00:00:35,084 --> 00:00:41,460
but it's not used much for domestic purposes now because it's highly toxic.

11
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:47,340
But the problem is that the amount of mercury in the environment's increasing.

12
00:00:47,340 --> 00:00:53,020
The main reason for this is the power plants used to produce electricity.

13
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:58,593
The main source of energy that most of them use is still coal,

14
00:00:58,593 --> 00:01:02,880
and when it's burned it releases mercury into the atmosphere.

15
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:07,110
Some of this gets deposited into lakes and rivers,

16
00:01:07,110 --> 00:01:14,630
and if it's ingested by a fish it's not excreted, it stays in the fish's body

17
00:01:14,630 --> 00:01:17,450
and it enters the food chain.

18
00:01:17,450 --> 00:01:24,192
So it's been known for some time that birds which eat fish may be

19
00:01:24,192 --> 00:01:24,673
affected,

20
00:01:24,673 --> 00:01:31,415
but what wasn't known until quite recently is that those that eat insects can

21
00:01:31,415 --> 00:01:32,860
also be affected.

22
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:40,796
So a woman called Claire Varian-Ramos is doing some research on how this is

23
00:01:40,796 --> 00:01:41,930
affecting birds.

24
00:01:41,930 --> 00:01:49,504
And rather than looking at how many birds are actually killed by mercury poisoning,

25
00:01:49,504 --> 00:01:52,750
she's looking for more subtle sub-effects .

26
00:01:52,750 --> 00:01:57,944
And these may be to do with the behaviour of the birds,

27
00:01:57,944 --> 00:02:03,138
or with the effect of mercury on the way their brain works,

28
00:02:03,138 --> 00:02:07,466
so whether it leads to problems with memory, for example.

29
00:02:07,466 --> 00:02:12,660
And she's particularly focusing on the effects of mercury on bird song.

30
00:02:12,660 --> 00:02:13,075
Now.

31
00:02:13,075 --> 00:02:18,882
the process of song learning happens at a particular stage in the birds' development,

32
00:02:18,882 --> 00:02:24,689
and what you may not know is that a young bird seems to acquire

33
00:02:24,689 --> 00:02:30,496
this skill by listening to the songs produced by its father, rather than by

34
00:02:30,496 --> 00:02:31,740
any other bird.

35
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:38,453
And Varian-Ramos has already found in her research that if young male birds are

36
00:02:38,453 --> 00:02:45,165
exposed to mercury, if they eat food contaminated with mercury, then the songs they

37
00:02:45,165 --> 00:02:49,960
produce aren't as complex as those produced by other birds.

38
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:56,752
So quite low-level exposure to mercury is likely to have an impact on male

39
00:02:56,752 --> 00:02:59,177
birds in a natural situation,

40
00:02:59,177 --> 00:03:04,514
because it can mean that they're less attractive to female birds,

41
00:03:04,514 --> 00:03:08,880
and so it can affect their chances of reproduction.

42
00:03:08,880 --> 00:03:15,135
Now the way she's carrying out this research is worth thinking about.

43
00:03:15,135 --> 00:03:20,870
She's using a mixture of studies using birds kept in laboratories,

44
00:03:20,870 --> 00:03:25,040
and studies carried out outdoors in the wild.

45
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:31,050
The lab-based studies have the advantage that you don't get all the variables you

46
00:03:31,050 --> 00:03:33,197
would in a natural setting,

47
00:03:33,197 --> 00:03:37,490
so the experimenter has a much higher level of control,

48
00:03:37,490 --> 00:03:43,500
and that means they can be more confident about their results in some ways.

49
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:47,608
And of course they don't have to worry about going out

50
00:03:47,608 --> 00:03:50,970
and finding the birds in order to observe them.

51
00:03:50,970 --> 00:03:55,808
So what are the implications here for humans?

52
00:03:55,808 --> 00:03:56,413
Well,

53
00:03:56,413 --> 00:04:04,880
because many birds are migratory, they may be transporting mercury far from contaminated sites.

54
00:04:04,880 --> 00:04:12,196
For example, it's been found that ducks who'd been feeding at a contaminated site

55
00:04:12,196 --> 00:04:17,422
were later shot by hunters over a thousand kilometres away,

56
00:04:17,422 --> 00:04:18,990
and presumably eaten.

57
00:04:18,990 --> 00:04:26,233
But these birds likely had mercury levels high enough to warrant concern for human

58
00:04:26,233 --> 00:04:26,750
consumption.

59
00:04:26,750 --> 00:04:32,836
In addition, going back to song learning by birds, we saw that this may

60
00:04:32,836 --> 00:04:35,010
be affected by mercury contamination.

61
00:04:35,010 --> 00:04:41,995
Well, we also know that in humans, mercury causes developmental delays in the acquisition

62
00:04:41,995 --> 00:04:42,993
of language,

63
00:04:42,993 --> 00:04:49,978
and in fact this process is very similar in the brain regions it involves

64
00:04:49,978 --> 00:04:53,470
and even the genes that are involved.

65
00:04:53,470 --> 00:04:58,846
But mercury contamination has other important implications for humans as well.

66
00:04:58,846 --> 00:05:05,689
It's now known that an unborn child can be affected if the food eaten

67
00:05:05,689 --> 00:05:09,599
by its mother contains high levels of mercury,

68
00:05:09,599 --> 00:05:13,020
and these effects can be quite substantial.

69
00:05:13,020 --> 00:05:21,087
In the end, it comes down to whether more value is placed on human economic wellbeing

70
00:05:21,087 --> 00:05:22,600
or environmental wellbeing.

71
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:28,388
It's true there are new regulations for mercury emissions from power plants,

72
00:05:28,388 --> 00:05:32,728
but these will need billions of dollars to implement,

73
00:05:32,728 --> 00:05:35,140
and increase costs for everyone.

74
00:05:35,140 --> 00:05:39,994
Some argue that's too much to pay to protect wild life.

75
00:05:39,994 --> 00:05:43,965
But as we've seen , the issues go beyond that ,

76
00:05:43,965 --> 00:05:49,260
and I think it's an issue we need to consider very carefully.
